WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.602
Coding Exercise 3 asks you to insert a specific new patient and verify the inserted row with RETURNING.

00:00:07.602 --> 00:00:11.247
The task gives you the medical record number, name, age, and gender.

00:00:11.247 --> 00:00:18.742
Start by naming the target columns, then make sure the values are in the same order.

00:00:18.742 --> 00:00:24.952
Because the medical record number must be unique, the insert should identify the new row clearly.

00:00:24.952 --> 00:00:27.942
Run and Check after you complete the statement.

00:00:27.942 --> 00:00:32.577
If PostgreSQL reports a constraint error, read the message carefully.

00:00:32.577 --> 00:00:37.232
It may mean a required value is missing, a value violates a check,

00:00:37.232 --> 00:00:40.047
or the unique key already exists.

00:00:40.047 --> 00:00:46.697
The goal is to practice a complete insert-and-verify pattern, not simply to make the error disappear.

00:00:46.697 --> 00:00:50.852
For this activity, make a prediction before you run anything.

00:00:50.852 --> 00:00:55.917
State what one output row should represent, which records you expect to remain,

00:00:55.917 --> 00:00:59.467
and what edge case is most likely to expose a mistake.

00:00:59.467 --> 00:01:03.612
Then run the SQL and compare the result with that prediction.

00:01:03.612 --> 00:01:10.677
If it does not match, debug the logic in small pieces instead of changing several clauses at once.

00:01:10.677 --> 00:01:14.847
This is the same habit you would use in a real analytics project:

00:01:14.847 --> 00:01:19.552
validate the meaning of the result, not only whether the statement executes.
